lesson.body:
A surety is discharged:

- by notice of revocation or death in a continuing guarantee (sections 130, 131);
- by **variance** in the terms of the contract between creditor and debtor without the surety's consent (section 133);
- by **release or discharge** of the principal debtor (section 134);
- when the creditor **compounds** with, **gives time** to, or **agrees not to sue** the debtor without the surety's consent (section 135);
- by the creditor's act or omission that **impairs the surety's eventual remedy** (section 139);
- by loss of security (section 141, to the extent of its value).

Rights of the surety:

- **Subrogation** (section 140): on payment, he steps into the creditor's shoes.
- **Benefit of securities** held by the creditor (section 141).
- **Implied promise of indemnity** by the principal debtor (section 145).
- **Contribution** among co-sureties: equally, unless otherwise agreed (section 146); in proportion to their limits if the limits differ (section 147).

Mutual rights are governed by the partnership deed; subject to it, section 13 provides:

| Clause | Default rule |
| --- | --- |
| 13(a) | No remuneration for taking part in the business |
| 13(b) | Profits and losses shared equally |
| 13(c) | Interest on capital, if agreed, payable only out of profits |
| 13(d) | Interest on advances beyond capital at 6% per annum |
| 13(e) | Indemnity for payments and liabilities in the ordinary and proper conduct of business or in an emergency |
| 13(f) | Partner must indemnify the firm for loss caused by his wilful neglect |

Other rights: take part in business (section 12(a)), be consulted, have access to books (section 12(d)). Ordinary matters are decided by majority; a change in the nature of business needs consent of all (section 12(c)).

**Implied authority** (section 19): every partner is an agent of the firm (section 18); an act done in the usual way to carry on business of the kind binds the firm. Unless usage or custom permits, implied authority does **not** extend to (section 19(2)):

- submit a dispute to arbitration;
- open a bank account in his own name on the firm's behalf;
- compromise or relinquish a claim of the firm;
- withdraw a suit filed by the firm;
- admit liability in a suit against the firm;
- acquire or transfer immovable property of the firm;
- enter into partnership on the firm's behalf.

Section 25: every partner is liable **jointly and severally** for all acts of the firm while he is a partner. Section 28: **holding out** - a person who represents himself, or knowingly allows himself to be represented, as a partner is liable to those who gave credit on that basis.

**Ultra vires**: an act beyond the objects of the MoA is ultra vires the company and **void**; it cannot be ratified even by all members. **Ashbury Railway Carriage and Iron Co. Ltd. v. Riche (1875)**. Acts ultra vires the directors but intra vires the company can be ratified by members.

**Constructive notice**: the MoA and AoA are public documents registered with the Registrar. Every person dealing with the company is deemed to know their contents. An outsider cannot plead ignorance of a restriction in them.

**Indoor management**: an outsider dealing in good faith is entitled to assume that the company's **internal procedures** (meetings, quorum, resolutions) have been properly complied with. **Royal British Bank v. Turquand (1856)**: the articles allowed directors to borrow if authorised by a resolution of the general meeting; the bank was entitled to assume the resolution had been passed. The doctrine protects outsiders from the harsh effect of constructive notice. Section 176 also provides that acts of a director are valid despite a defect later discovered in his appointment.

Exceptions to indoor management:

- the outsider had **actual or constructive knowledge** of the irregularity;
- **suspicion** of irregularity, or negligence in not enquiring;
- **forgery**: a forged document is a nullity;
- act outside the apparent authority of the officer;
- the outsider is an insider (e.g. a director who should know the procedure).

Offence under section 138 arises when:

- a cheque is drawn on an account **maintained by the drawer**;
- for discharge, wholly or partly, of a **legally enforceable debt or liability**;
- it is returned unpaid because funds are **insufficient** or it exceeds the arrangement;
- it was presented within **six months** of its date or within its period of validity, whichever is earlier (validity is currently three months as directed by RBI);
- the payee or holder in due course gives a **written notice** demanding payment within **30 days** of receiving information of dishonour from the bank;
- the drawer **fails to pay within 15 days** of receipt of the notice.

Punishment: imprisonment up to **two years**, or fine up to **twice the amount** of the cheque, or both.

Procedure:

- Complaint in writing by the payee or holder in due course within **one month** of the cause of action, i.e. expiry of the 15 days (section 142); the court may condone delay for sufficient cause.
- Tried by a court not inferior to a Metropolitan Magistrate or Judicial Magistrate of the first class; summary trial (section 143).
- **Interim compensation** up to **20%** of the cheque amount (section 143A).
- In appeal against conviction, the court may order deposit of at least **20%** of the fine or compensation (section 148).
- Offences under the Act are **compoundable** (section 147).
- Section 141: where the drawer is a company, every person in charge of and responsible for the business is also liable, unless he proves lack of knowledge or due diligence.

Timeline example: cheque dated 1 March, dishonour memo received 10 April; notice must go by 10 May; notice received 14 May; payment due by 29 May; cause of action arises 30 May; complaint by 29 June.
